  padding: 1rem;
  background-color: #f3f5f8;
}
`
			},
			checks: [
				{
					id: 'spec-section',
					kind: 'exists',
					selector: '#api-spec #openapi'
				},
				{
					id: 'no-blanks',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#openapi',
					matches: '^[^…]*$'
				},
				{
					id: 'version',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#openapi',
					matches: '(?:^|\\n)\\s*openapi:\\s*3\\.\\d\\.\\d'
				},
				{
					id: 'servers-https',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#openapi',
					matches: 'url:\\s*https://\\S+'
				},
				{
					id: 'plural-nouns',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#openapi',
					matches: '(?:^|\\n)\\s{2}/[a-z][a-z-]*s:\\s*(?:\\n|$)'
				},
				{
					id: 'no-verbs',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#openapi',
					matches: '^(?![\\s\\S]*\\n\\s{2}/(?:get|create|add|delete|update|remove|list|fetch|send|save)[A-Za-z-]*[:/])'
				},
				{
					id: 'one-item-path',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#openapi',
					matches: '(?:^|\\n)\\s{2}/[a-z][a-z-]*s/\\{\\w+\\}:'
				},
				{
					id: 'get-operation',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#openapi',
					matches: '(?:^|\\n)\\s{4}get:'
				},
				{
					id: 'post-creates',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#openapi',
					matches: '(?:^|\\n)\\s{4}post:[\\s\\S]*[\'"]?20[12][\'"]?\\s*:'
				},
				{
					id: 'statuses',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#openapi',
					matches: '^(?=[\\s\\S]*[\'"]?200[\'"]?\\s*:)(?=[\\s\\S]*[\'"]?400[\'"]?\\s*:)(?=[\\s\\S]*[\'"]?404[\'"]?\\s*:)'
				},
				{
					id: 'error-schema',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#openapi',
					matches: 'Error:[\\s\\S]*code:[\\s\\S]*message:|Error:[\\s\\S]*message:[\\s\\S]*code:'
				},
				{
					id: 'required-fields',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#openapi',
					matches: 'required:\\s*\\[[^\\]]+\\]|required:\\s*\\n\\s*-\\s*\\w+'
				},
				{
					id: 'https-personal-data',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#openapi',
					matches: '^(?=[\\s\\S]*(?:HTTPS|https))(?=[\\s\\S]*[Pp]ersonal)'
				}
			]
		},
		quiz: [
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'A page requests GET /items?delete=2, and afterwards item 2 is gone. What is wrong?',
				options: [
					'GET must be safe and change nothing: deleting needs DELETE',
					'Nothing: you choose the address yourself',
					'The list should have been read with POST'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. GET is defined as safe: anybody may call it, any number of times (a browser, a search robot, an extension). If it deletes something, one curious robot wipes your data.',
					'No: a free choice of address does not cancel the rules of the methods. Caches, robots and security depend on them.',
					'No: reading a list is exactly what GET is for.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'match',
				prompt: 'Match each HTTP method with its usual meaning in an API',
				pairs: [
					['GET', 'read a resource, changing nothing'],
					['POST', 'create a new resource or perform an action'],
					['PUT', 'replace a resource entirely'],
					['PATCH', 'change some fields of a resource'],
					['DELETE', 'remove a resource']
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'Which address is best for a list of offerings in the REST style?',
				options: ['GET /items', 'GET /getAllItems', 'POST /items/list'],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. The address names the resource (offerings, in the plural) and the method expresses the action. That makes an API predictable: /items, /items/7, /requests.',
					'No: a verb in the address repeats what the GET method already says.',
					'No: reading takes GET, and POST means “create”.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'An inquiry has been accepted and created on the server. Which status code is expected?',
				options: ['201 Created, usually with a Location header', '200 OK with no explanation', '404 Not Found'],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. 201 says a resource was created, and Location gives its address. Use 202 when the inquiry was accepted but will be processed later.',
					'It is allowed, but 201 is more informative: it says specifically that something was created.',
					'No: 404 means something is missing, not that it was created.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'multiple',
				prompt: 'What is useful in an API’s error message?',
				options: [
					'The right status code (400, 404, 409…)',
					'A short machine-readable error code',
					'Text a person can read, with no secrets in it',
					'A full stack trace and the database password'
				],
				answer: [0, 1, 2],
				why: [
					'Yes: the code tells the class of trouble at once.',
					'Yes: a client program uses it to decide what to do (retry, highlight a field, sign out).',
					'Yes: the text helps the developer and the visitor, but must not reveal how the system is built.',
					'No: that is how passwords and the layout of a server leak. Details go in the log, not in the response.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'What is an OpenAPI file for?',
				options: [
					'It describes the API in a machine-readable form: documentation is built from it, requests are checked against it and clients are generated',
					'It makes the server faster',
					'It replaces the database'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. One file is a contract between the people who build the server and the people who use it. Interactive documentation (Swagger UI), code stubs and tests come out of it automatically.',
					'No: it does not speed a server up, it describes the server’s interface.',
					'No: it does not replace a database.'
				]
			}
		],
		cards: [
			{
				id: 'server-rest-what',
				front: 'What is REST?',
				back: 'A style of designing web APIs from Roy Fielding’s 2000 dissertation: resources with addresses, HTTP methods as actions, status codes, and responses that do not depend on earlier requests. Most often it means JSON over HTTP.'
			},
			{
				id: 'server-rest-methods',
				front: 'Which HTTP methods are there, and what do they mean?',
				back: 'GET: read (safe). POST: create. PUT: replace entirely. PATCH: change part. DELETE: remove. GET, PUT and DELETE are idempotent: repeating them gives the same result.'
			},
			{
				id: 'server-rest-codes',
				front: 'Which API status codes should you know?',
				back: '200 success, 201 created, 204 success with no body, 400 bad request, 401 not signed in, 403 forbidden, 404 not found, 409 conflict, 429 too many requests, 500 server error.'
			},
			{
				id: 'server-api-errors',
				front: 'How should API errors look?',
				back: 'The same everywhere: a status code, a short error code and readable text (the fields code and message, or the RFC 9457 format). No secrets and no stack trace.'
			},
			{
				id: 'server-openapi',
				front: 'What is OpenAPI?',
				back: 'A format for describing an HTTP API in a YAML or JSON file: paths, methods, parameters, responses, data schemas. Documentation (Swagger UI), clients and tests come from it. It grew out of Swagger (2011).'
			}
		],
		sources: [
			{
				title: 'Fielding: Architectural Styles and the Design of Network-based Software Architectures, chapter 5 (REST)',
				url: 'https://ics.uci.edu/~fielding/pubs/dissertation/rest_arch_style.htm',
				licence: 'link-only',
				kind: 'book',
				lang: 'en'
			},
			{
				title: 'RFC 9110: HTTP Semantics',
				url: 'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110',
				licence: 'link-only',
				kind: 'spec',
				lang: 'en'
			},
			{
				title: 'OpenAPI Specification',
				url: 'https://spec.openapis.org/oas/latest.html',
				licence: 'link-only',
				kind: 'spec',
				lang: 'en'
			},
			{
				title: 'MDN: HTTP request methods',
				url: 'https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Methods',
				licence: 'CC-BY-SA',
				kind: 'docs',
				lang: 'en'
			},
			{
				title: 'MDN: HTTP response status codes',
				url: 'https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Status',
				licence: 'CC-BY-SA',
				kind: 'docs',
				lang: 'en'
			},
			{
				title: 'RFC 9457: Problem Details for HTTP APIs',
				url: 'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457',
				licence: 'link-only',
				kind: 'spec',
				lang: 'en'
			}
		],
		videos: [
			{
				title: 'What is a REST API? (IBM Technology)',
				url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lsMQRaeKNDk',
				licence: 'proprietary-link',
				kind: 'video',
				lang: 'en'
			}
		],
		guide: {
			intro: 'You’ll describe the API the way real projects do: with a file in the OpenAPI standard. The lesson has an example. Put in your own paths and fields, and keep to the rules: nouns, the right methods, honest status codes.',
			hints: [
				'The start: openapi: 3.1.0, info with a title and a version, and servers: - url: https://riverbend.example/api/v1. Indentation is two spaces a level.',
				'Paths are indented two spaces and start with a slash: /items: and /items/{id}: and /requests:. The method goes two more spaces in: get: or post:.',
				'Responses: responses: and under it the codes in quotes: “200”, “404”, and for the inquiry “201” and “400”. Move the shared error into components: schemas: Error: with the fields code and message.',
				'For the inquiry: required: [name, phone] and a sentence in description: “personal data is accepted only over HTTPS”.'
			],
			mistakes: {
				'spec-section': 'You need a section with the id api-spec holding a pre with the id openapi. Put it back.',
				'no-blanks': 'The description still has the “…”. Replace the whole block with your API description.',
				version: 'The description starts with the line openapi: 3.1.0.',
				'servers-https': 'servers needs an HTTPS address: - url: https://riverbend.example/api/v1.',
				'plural-nouns': 'Paths should be plural nouns: /items, /requests.',
				'no-verbs': 'There must be no verbs in the paths (getItems, createRequest, delete…): the method says the action.',
				'one-item-path': 'You need the path of one resource with a parameter: /items/{id}.',
				'get-operation': 'You need a read operation: get: inside a path.',
				'post-creates': 'An inquiry is created with post and answers with the code “201” (or “202”).',
				statuses: 'Describe the 200, 400 and 404 answers.',
				'error-schema': 'The shared error format: components, then schemas, then Error with the fields code and message.',
				'required-fields': 'List the required fields of the inquiry: required: [name, phone].',
				'https-personal-data': 'Write that personal data is accepted only over HTTPS (in the description of the inquiry).'
			},
			outro: 'Your project now has a contract: any programmer (and any AI model) can read this file and know how to talk to it. Paste the text into the Swagger editor (editor.swagger.io) and it draws interactive documentation.'
		},
		say: {
			API: 'A P I',
			REST: 'rest',
			OpenAPI: 'open A P I',
			Swagger: 'swagger',
			YAML: 'yammel',
			JSON: 'jay-son',
